\begin{question}
\textbf{Association and causation}

In a sample of primary school children, shoe size and reading skills are strongly positively correlated. Which statement is correct?
\begin{answerlist}
\item The correlation is most likely driven by a confounder: older children have larger feet \emph{and} read better.
\item Larger shoes cause better reading skills.
\item Better reading skills cause larger feet.
\item A strong correlation always implies a causal effect in at least one direction.
\item Since the correlation is strong, the relationship cannot be explained by a third variable.
\end{answerlist}
\end{question}

\begin{solution}
\begin{answerlist}
\item True. Age influences both variables and creates the association (confounding). Among children of the same age, we would expect almost no correlation.
\item False. The correlation only shows a statistical association, not a causal effect.
\item False. The correlation only shows a statistical association, not a causal effect.
\item False. Association is not causation; a confounder can create a strong correlation without any causal effect between the two variables.
\item False. Confounders can create arbitrarily strong correlations.
\end{answerlist}
\end{solution}

